o impact  more than just a fraction of the  population i think that's where we can  move the field forward very quickly  well i want to leave  we have a question from the audience um  dr ryan roberts asks  aside from safety how would a lack of  efficacy  in a canine study affect the approval  process  so i'm going to extend that question i'm  going to first toss it to greg but then  i'm going to ask mark the comment is  how would it affect thinking in industry  um with a negative study but greg from  uh from a regulatory standpoint uh any  any uh red flags there i guess i would  just  uh question or ask for some  clarification about the  uh approval process uh in the uh the  human uh or pediatric setting or or the  or the canine  uh approval you know how would know  thinking about a pediatric study or  pediatric approval  yeah i think i think how it would affect  uh is is probably it would have uh  an impact uh not so much on the approval  process uh as it would uh  on the decision-making with respect to  whether a product should or should not  be  evaluated or be considered for  development  in the pediatric situation  and i and i think uh if there's uh uh  and  i guess it also depends on the um  definition of lack of efficacy  uh you know i think if uh um the  specific uh  um target uh is not uh altered  from a pharmacodynamic perspective  um and the  function of the drug is really in  question  um then i think that would sort of lower  um the interest if you will um in  proceeding to uh pediatric development  and mark what are your thoughts the  unintended consequences you get a  a negative study in in in a  dog clinical trial um is that going to  uh  how impactful do you think that might be  yeah i mean it will have a pact  obviously the  fidelity of of exactly  you know the reason it failed was it a  pharmacokinetic was it a pharmacodynamic  was it a  salvage pathway and are those the now  how similar is the  dog pathways to the the human in terms  of  how much can you use that data as i've  said it's already  a bit of an uphill climb um nudging  leadership towards pediatric studies  that obviously are not going to have a  huge economic  impact on the company and negative data  obviously probably takes a little bit of  wind out of what is already a pretty  tiny sale  so i don't think it would be helpful in  some ways though the race for children  act  kind of will help negate some of that  because  if there's still a sense from the  regulatory authorities that you still  need to study that pathway to meet your  obligations  for an adult indication then  you may be back on the table whereas in  the older systems that might have been  all the justification  they needed to end the pediatric  evaluation at least early on and  consider it maybe later in the life  cycle  of the drug so i mean it's obviously a  complicated issue that's dependent on  multiple factors  but and and it may be a good thing it  may be that the negative results in a  dog  are an opportunity to save a whole bunch  of kids from getting a drug that it  turns out are not going to be effective  one of the things we're trying to do  certainly at bms and i think most  companies have taken this approach  and i think academics are getting more  in line with is  we say that a trial fails and often the  question  is we understand when a trial is  successful because you see the success  but we often don't know why a trial  fails and it therefore makes it hard to  make a decision  about what to do next or instead of and  so one of the things we're trying to do  is build in  um not for the five percent of trials  that are going to be successful but the  95 that are going to be negative  so that we know why it's negative um and  can therefore make an intelligent  decision  about that house how that would affect  future development  whether that be pediatric or adult  well thanks mark well we're at the top  of the hour i i want to thank  all our panelists for our very robust  discussion  certainly thank the organizers for  allowing us to have this session and  perhaps most importantly again thank dan  for telling us all why we're here and  why we have to do this  so with that thanks everyone and enjoy  the rest of this really important  meeting
 

